{#
calendar/_month_shell.html.twig — what a month LOOKS like, with nothing in it.

Extracted from _view_month.html.twig when the public shared-calendar page grew a
month view of its own. The two calendars draw different things in a cell — one
opens an editor on an event, the other prints a redacted block that has no event
behind it to open — but the grid itself is the same object in both: a weekday
strip, six rows that share the height evenly, hairline gutters, and a cell that
says whether it is today, a weekend, a spill-in from a neighbouring month, or
outside the range altogether.

It is a shell rather than a copy because that last sentence is the visual
identity of this application's calendar. Copied into a second file it drifts on
the first change to either, and the public page ends up looking like a calendar
from a different product — which is exactly the complaint that produced it.

Always six weeks, never five-or-six. Callers hand over 42 keys from the Monday
on or before the first, so the grid does not change height between months — a
calendar that reflows as you page through it is disorienting, and it means the
surrounding layout never has to reserve for the taller case.

`grid-rows-6` is what makes the six weeks share the height evenly instead of each
row shrinking to its busiest cell. The authenticated calendar pairs it with
`h-full` on a flex parent; a page with no fixed height passes a row-height
minimum instead, which is what shellRowsClass is for.

Embed it, do not include it:

    {% embed 'calendar/_month_shell.html.twig' with { ... } %}
        {% block cell_actions %}…{% endblock %}
        {% block cell_body %}…{% endblock %}
    {% endembed %}

Variables:
  dayKeys          list<string>  42 keys, Y-m-d, Monday first
  days             map           keyed by those; a MISSING key means the cell is
                                 outside the range the caller is publishing,
                                 which is not the same as an empty one
  anchorMonth      string|int    the `n` of the month on screen — spill-in days
                                 are the ones that do not match
  today            string        Y-m-d in the view's own zone, never the reader's
  shellOuterClass  string        optional, the frame's own sizing
  shellRowsClass   string        optional, how the six rows get their height
  shellCellClass   string        optional, extra classes on every cell

Blocks, both rendered inside the cell loop and both able to read `dayKey`,
`dayEntries`, `isToday`, `isWeekend`, `isOtherMonth` and `isOutside`:
  cell_actions  the top-right corner of a cell
  cell_body     everything under the date
#}

{% set shellOuterClass = shellOuterClass|default('h-full min-h-0 flex flex-col') %}
{% set shellRowsClass  = shellRowsClass|default('flex-1 min-h-0') %}
{% set shellCellClass  = shellCellClass|default('') %}

<div class="{{ shellOuterClass }}">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-7 shrink-0 border-b border-line">
        {% for dayKey in dayKeys|slice(0, 7) %}
            <div class="px-2 py-1.5 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint">
                {{ dayKey|intl_date('EEE') }}
            </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>

    {# Named in the markup rather than left to a class list. `data-day` is the
       spelling the time-grid's columns already use for the same idea, and it is
       what lets a test say "this is a month grid and it has 42 cells" without
       asserting against Tailwind classes, which are layout and are allowed to
       change. #}
    <div class="{{ shellRowsClass }} grid grid-cols-7 grid-rows-6 gap-px bg-line"
         data-calendar-grid="month">
        {% for dayKey in dayKeys %}
            {# A key the caller did not supply is a day it is not publishing.
               The authenticated calendar always supplies all 42, so this is
               false there and the branch costs it nothing; the shared page uses
               it to keep "outside the window" distinguishable from "free",
               which is the one thing that page must never confuse. #}
            {% set isOutside = days[dayKey] is not defined %}
            {% set dayEntries = days[dayKey]|default([]) %}
            {% set isToday = dayKey == today %}
            {% set isWeekend = dayKey|date('N') >= 6 %}
            {% set isOtherMonth = dayKey|date('n') != anchorMonth %}

            {# The spill-in days are dimmed as a whole cell, not just by their
               date number: a neighbouring month's events rendered at full
               strength read as part of this month. #}
            {# Which way the day's peek may open, decided here because only
               this loop knows where the cell is. A panel anchored to the top of
               a cell in the last two rows grows off the bottom of the grid, and
               one anchored left in the last two columns grows off the right —
               neither of which CSS can notice on its own, and both of which the
               row and column index answer exactly. The caller puts
               `cellPeekAnchor` on whatever it opens; see _view_month. #}
            {# Which EDGE the peek lets go of. It is pinned to all four at rest
               so it covers exactly the box it would have occupied in flow, and
               opening releases ONE of them — never repositions it, which is
               what keeps the chips inside where the pointer found them.

               Vertical only, and that is the anti-jump rule doing real work
               rather than a simplification. A peek that also widened re-wrapped
               the titles it already held: the first chip in a cell went from
               two lines to one as it opened, and the chip under it — which is
               as likely as not the one being pointed at — jumped 16px upwards
               to meet it. Growing down alone reflows nothing that was already
               drawn.

               Up instead of down for the last two rows, because there is
               nothing under them to grow into: the grid is exactly six rows of
               the pane and a panel hanging off the bottom is clipped by it.
               Those cells DO shift their contents as they open, by however much
               is revealed. It is the one case with no better answer. #}
            {# `@min-[5.5rem]:` on all of it, because a peek is worth opening
               only where there is something behind the truncation to reveal.
               Below that the chips have already given up their titles and a
               wider box would show the same bars and times in more space. The
               day is still one tap away — that is what "N more" and the date
               itself are for. #}
            {% set cellPeekAnchor = (loop.index0 // 7) >= 4
                ? '@min-[5.5rem]:group-hover/cell:top-auto'
                : '@min-[5.5rem]:group-hover/cell:bottom-auto' %}

            {# `relative` is the peek's anchor and `hover:z-40` is what puts it
               over the days around it — a cell is a grid item, so without a
               number the six cells after it in source paint on top. The clip
               has to lift with it: it is what hides the surplus entries the
               rest of the time, and it would go on hiding them.

               **`@container`, so a chip can ask how wide the day it is on
               actually is.** Seven columns of a 360px phone are 42px each, and
               at 42px a title is one letter and an ellipsis — which says less
               than the blank space it is printed on. The chip already knows how
               to give up its words in order; it only ever lacked something to
               ask. See _event_chip, where the two steps are set out, and note
               that no viewport breakpoint could have answered this: the same
               360px window is a month in a pane and a month on a page, and the
               cell is a seventh of whichever it is. #}
            <div data-day="{{ dayKey }}"
                 {% if isOutside %}data-day-unpublished{% endif %}
                 class="group/cell @container relative hover:z-40 hover:overflow-visible
                        min-h-0 min-w-0 flex flex-col p-1 gap-0.5 overflow-hidden {{ shellCellClass }}
                        {{ isOutside
                            ? 'bg-sunken/20'
                            : (isToday ? 'bg-accent/5' : (isWeekend ? 'bg-sunken/40' : 'bg-surface')) }}
                        {{ isOtherMonth ? 'opacity-55' : '' }}">

                <div class="flex items-center justify-between shrink-0 px-0.5">
                    {# The number is a block of its own so a caller can make it
                       the way into its day. It is the affordance every other
                       calendar puts there, and this grid had none: the only
                       route in was "N more", which appeared on busy days and
                       nowhere else. The shared page leaves it as text — there is
                       no day to open on a page that publishes a window. #}
                    {% block cell_date %}
                        {% if isToday %}
                            <span class="w-5 h-5 flex items-center justify-center rounded-full
                                         bg-accent text-accent-ink text-xs font-semibold tabular-nums">
                                {{ dayKey|date('j') }}
                            </span>
                        {% else %}
                            <span class="text-xs tabular-nums {{ isOtherMonth or isOutside ? 'text-ink-faint' : 'text-ink-muted' }}">
                                {{ dayKey|date('j') }}
                            </span>
                        {% endif %}
                    {% endblock %}

                    {% block cell_actions %}{% endblock %}
                </div>

                {% block cell_body %}{% endblock %}
            </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>
</div>
